Each time I play a healer, I wish I had better control over my pets.
No pet is stupid enough to nibble a cockatrice, but my lemming-
mannered pets will attack gray molds (in sporkhack) and blue jellies.
My pets also often get distracted by monsters *I don't care about
killing*: I don't want my dog munching gnomes while I'm getting MAULED
BY A ****ing LEOCROTTA, for pete's sake.

That said, I'd appreciate a (limited) resource that would allow better
control over my pets. One could apply a spray bottle of skunk musk to
a monster to repel pets from attacking it, and apply a spray bottle of
fluorescent dye to make invisible monsters temporarily visible and
make pets want to attack them. I'm not sure *why* pets would attack
something fluorescent, but then, any pet you get responds to a stupid
tin whistle, even if it was a stray thirty seconds ago. Maybe pets are
"trained" to attack bright yellow things. Additionally, pets may
attack slightly higher-level creatures than usual, and sprayed
peacefuls get angry, making the dye something to be used with
restraint.

The bottles would each contain a limited number of charges.
Additionally, applying skunk musk to yourself will temporarily
frighten some monsters, but shopkeepers will utterly refuse you
admittance into their shops until the horrible smell wears off.

"Hello, welcome to Uvernavik's general store - wait, did *you* do
that? Ugh, get out of here before I call hazmat!"

A dead, stinky monster will also be temporarily unappetizing to your
pet, keeping Fido from eating the gnomes while you get mauled by Mr.
Leocrotta.

"Fido sniffs the gnome corpse and flinches in disgust."

I can think of tons of uses for the spray bottles. There's the gnome/
leocrotta scenario mentioned earler. There's the common invisible
nymph/invisible gnome lord early game scenario. And there's the "I'm
surrounded by soldier ants" scenario, in which our hero decides to
spray himself with skunk stink to provide a way of repelling the ants
*as he runs away* for five or six turns rather than a stationary
defense such as an engraved E-square. What do you think?



